-- title:
--   Typed primitives modulo [q] span two lines
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, let $j_0\colon \mathbb{Z}_{q^2} = W(\mathbb{F}_{q^2}) \to k$ be a ring homomorphism, and let $X_0$ be a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $k$ relative to $j_0$ of height $4$: a $2$-dimensional commutative formal group law $F = X_0.F$ together with an action $a \mapsto X_0.\mathrm{act}\,a$ of $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$ by endomorphisms of $F$ and a series $X_0.\varpi$ with $\varpi\circ\varpi = [q]$ and $\varpi\circ[a] = [\sigma a]\circ\varpi$, satisfying the specialness and height conditions of the structure. Write $I \subseteq k[[X_1,X_2]]$ for the ideal generated by the two components of $[q]_F = F.\mathrm{nthSeries}\,q$, and $I_2$ for the ideal of $k[[X,Y]]$ generated by the components of $[q]_F(X)$ and of $[q]_F(Y)$; call $g$ primitive if $g(0)=0$ and $\partial_F g = g(F(X,Y)) - g(X) - g(Y) \in I_2$. Assume there are primitives $e,e'$ that are independent modulo $I$ (if $c e + c' e' \in I$ with $c,c' \in k$ then $c = c' = 0$) and span all primitives modulo $I$. The conclusion produces primitives $g_0, g_1$, neither in $I$, with $g_0\circ[a] \equiv j_0(a)\,g_0$ and $g_1\circ[a] \equiv j_0(\sigma a)\,g_1 \pmod I$ for all $a \in \mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$, such that every primitive $h$ with $h\circ[a] \equiv j_0(a)\,h \pmod I$ for all $a$ satisfies $h \equiv c\,g_0 \pmod I$ for some $c \in k$, and every primitive $h$ with $h\circ[a] \equiv j_0(\sigma a)\,h \pmod I$ for all $a$ satisfies $h \equiv c\,g_1 \pmod I$ for some $c \in k$.
--
--   This is the eigenspace decomposition of the two-dimensional space of primitives modulo $[q]$ — the additive dual of the $q$-torsion of a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module — into one line of type $j_0$ and one line of type $j_0 \circ \sigma$ under the $\mathbb{Z}_{q^2}$-action. It feeds the refinement `exists_primitives_mod_nthSeries_typed_forall_subst_addVia_act_of_finrank_eq_two`, in which the two typed generators are normalised further.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_SpecialFormalODModule_exists_primitives_mod_nthSeries_typed_lines_of_finrank_eq_two.lean

